window.docContents[35].resolve({"/Gamma-Function/Gamma-Function-Inequalities/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Gamma-Function--Gamma-Function-Inequalities--___5___6-Inequalities--___5___6_LPAR_i_RPAR_-Real-Variables":{"contents":"Throughout this subsection, x>0. Real powers in the declarations below use Mathlib's\nreal-power operation.\n\n1<(2\\pi)^{-1/2}x^{1/2-x}e^x\\Gamma(x)<e^{1/(12x)}.After division by the leading term in Stirling's formula, the Gamma function is strictly larger\nthan one and strictly smaller than the elementary exponential correction e^{1/(12x)}.\n\n\\frac1{\\Gamma(x)}+\\frac1{\\Gamma(1/x)}\\le 2.The reciprocal Gamma values at the positive reciprocal arguments x and 1/x have sum at\nmost two.\n\n\\frac1{\\Gamma(x)^2}+\\frac1{\\Gamma(1/x)^2}\\le 2.The corresponding sum of squared reciprocal Gamma values is also at most two.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Gamma Function\u0009Gamma-Function Inequalities\u0009§5.6 Inequalities","header":"§5.6(i) Real Variables","id":"/Gamma-Function/Gamma-Function-Inequalities/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Gamma-Function--Gamma-Function-Inequalities--___5___6-Inequalities--___5___6_LPAR_i_RPAR_-Real-Variables"}});